    <string name="lock_consent_body" translatable="false">Double tap to lock uses Android\'s accessibility service. Android tells Parem\'s service when you tap inside Parem. The service ignores every tap except the one on its own invisible lock area, and when that tap comes it asks Android to lock the screen. It gets no events from other apps and cannot read your screen. Nothing is stored or sent anywhere. You can turn it off any time in Accessibility settings.</string>
    <string name="lock_consent_accept" translatable="false">Agree and open settings</string>
    <string name="lock_consent_decline" translatable="false">Not now</string>
    <string name="lock_consent_restricted" translatable="false">If the switch is greyed out: App info > menu > Allow restricted settings.</string>
    <string name="lock_service_off_title" translatable="false">Lock gesture is off</string>
    <string name="lock_service_off_body" translatable="false">Parem\'s accessibility service is no longer running, so your lock gesture cannot lock the screen. Android can turn it off after an app update, a crash, or when a security setting restricts accessibility services. Turn it back on in Accessibility settings, or pick another gesture action in Parem\'s gesture settings.</string>
    <string name="lock_service_off_toast" translatable="false">Can\'t lock: accessibility is off. Other options in Settings › Gestures</string>
    <string name="lock_admin_option" translatable="false">Lock with device admin instead (needs PIN after every lock)</string>
    <string name="lock_admin_off_row" translatable="false">Lock without accessibility (PIN after every lock)</string>
    <string name="lock_admin_on_row" translatable="false">Lock uses device admin (PIN after each lock): turn off</string>
